  [1mMy recommendation: in-app feed, with the OS-notification layer written as a thin optional adapter behind the same [0m
  [1minterface.[0m The feed is the product; OS toasts are a one-file enhancement you can switch on once the feed feels right,
  and keeping them behind an adapter means the permission mess stays in one place and never touches the scheduler.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should notifications surface? → In-app feed, adapter for OS later (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the stack — the last fork that constrains the design, and one the empty [38;5;153mindex.html[39m leaves wide open.

  - [1mVanilla JS + ES modules, no build step[0m — open the file and it runs. Keeps the zero-infrastructure promise literal
    and makes the store/log/scheduler modules plain, testable functions. Costs you manual DOM updates; a notification
    feed with unread counts is real reactive state, and hand-rolled rendering gets tedious around the third view.
  - [1mVite + a small framework (React/Preact/Svelte)[0m — the feed, the unread badge, and the task list are exactly what
    declarative rendering is good at. Costs a build step, a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, and a dev server between you and the page.
  - [1mVanilla + a tiny reactive helper[0m — a ~50-line subscribe/render loop of our own. Avoids the dependency but means
    maintaining a miniature framework, which is usually a worse deal than either neighbor.

  [1mMy recommendation: Vite + Preact.[0m The reactive surface here is real, not hypothetical — unread counts, a live-ticking
  feed, and task edits all updating together — and Preact keeps the dependency small. If you'd rather this stay a file
  you can open with no toolchain at all, vanilla is a legitimate choice and I'll design the modules so the UI layer is
  the only thing that would change.
